## What Are Associated Types?

```xiom
pub interface Iterator {
  type Item;  // Associated type: what this iterator yields

  fn next(self: &mut Self) -> Option[Self::Item];
  fn size_hint(self: &Self) -> (Int, Option[Int]);
}
```

`Item` is an associated type — each implementor specifies what it is:

```xiom
impl Iterator for VecIter[T] {
  type Item = T;  // VecIter[Int] yields Int, VecIter[Str] yields Str

  fn next(self: &mut VecIter[T]) -> Option[T] { /* ... */ }
  fn size_hint(self: &VecIter[T]) -> (Int, Option[Int]) { /* ... */ }
}
```

### Associated Types vs Generic Parameters

```xiom
// With generics: callers must specify the type
fn interface Parser[Input, Output] {
  fn parse(input: Input) -> Output;
}

// With associated types: one type naturally determines the other
fn interface Parser {
  type Input;
  type Output;
  fn parse(input: Self::Input) -> Self::Output;
}
```

### Using Associated Types in Constraints
```xiom
fn collect_items[I](iter: &mut I) -> Vec[I::Item]
  where I: Iterator;
{
  var result = Vec[I::Item]();
  while let Some(item) = iter.next() {
    result.push(item);
  }
  return result;
}
```

